sec.p:
The system’s general response to surface perturbations differs notably between changes in albedo and hydrology (Fig. 1a–f and supplement SF5). Nonetheless, the decisive shifts in the surface energy balance are invariably driven by radiative processes, with the decreases in net surface shortwave (SW) radiation being the only factor that is consistent with the cooling in all simulations (sec. 2.2). Such a response agrees well with our understanding of albedo changes but not with the local effects of shifts in hydrology. The latter are dominated by the evaporative cooling associated with changes in the latent heat flux, hence, by non-radiative processes12–14. On larger spatial scales, however, the increase in the latent heat flux is offset by opposing shifts in the sensible heat fluxes, with the turbulent exchange often removing less energy from the surface (Fig. 1d, f and supplement SF3). This compensation arises mainly because evaporative cooling lowers the surface temperature while subsequent condensational heating warms the atmosphere, jointly reducing the surface-atmosphere temperature gradient that drives the sensible heat flux. And in case of a net increase in the turbulent fluxes, the resultant energy deficit is compensated by an increase in atmospheric counterradiation (Fig. 1e, k). As a result, the overall effect of non-radiative processes is largely limited to the redistribution of energy between unperturbed- and perturbed regions (Fig. 1g–l).Fig. 1Surface energy balance.
Panels a–f show 50-year mean, zonally averaged changes in the surface-energy fluxes (EN) resulting from increases in surface albedo (a–c) and water availability (d–f), with perturbed regions indicated on the x-axes. SW net is net surface shortwave radiation [orange], LW net is net surface longwave radiation [red], (H+LE) is the sum of turbulent fluxes [blue], and OLHU is ocean and land heat uptake [brown]. All fluxes are defined as positive downward except OLHU, which is defined as positive upward. Consequently, a positive value in the panels always corresponds to an increase of energy at the surface: 0 = sw net + lw net + (H + LE) + OLHU. Shading indicates the two dominant changes in the perturbed region, and solid lines indicate the less pronounced effects. Circles show the initial perturbation (pert.; either the prescribed albedo change multiplied by the incoming shortwave radiation from the control simulation [a-c] or the simulated change in latent heat flux [d–f]). Panels g–l show changes in specific humidity (shading) resulting from changes in surface albedo (g–i) and hydrology (j–l). Gray lines indicate changes in the low-altitude cloud cover (clouds (low); outer, right y-axis), purple lines in surface downwelling shortwave radiation (sw dwn; inner, right y-axis) and blue lines in the contribution of non-radiative processes to the atmospheric counterradiation (lw dwn non-rad.*; inner, right y-axis). The latter is approximated by comparing the simulated changes in counterradiation with temperature-based estimates (indicated by asterisks) that neglect changes in the atmospheric heat uptake due to changes in the turbulent fluxes and heat advection: \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
