In the last section (2.3), we attempt to estimate the climate impact of land-surface perturbations or combination of perturbations considering only the changes in the surface properties they entail (in case of water availability we consider the change in evapotranspiration directly), using sets of region-specific sensitivity parameters. These parameters are designed in a way that allows us to test a number of hypothesis that we make based on our analysis in the preceding sections. Our main hypothesis is that the overall (biophysical) climate impact (Δx) of any land surface change similarly arises due to and scales with the resulting change in planetary albedo (ΔalbTOA). Here, our findings suggest that land surface changes notably modify the planetary albedo only via changes in surface brightness or in the extent of the low-altitude cloud cover, with changes in the latter being mainly determined by shifts in Bowen ratio. Therefore, the overall response to any land surface change is assumed to be proportional to the changes in surface albedo (Δalbsrf) and evapotranspiration (Δevp) it entails, with the proportionality (sx,evp, sx,alb) being a function of the extent and sensitivity of the low-altitude cloud cover (cld): 3\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
