In this part of the analysis, we also attempt to estimate the climate effects due to changes in surface roughness (rgh), assuming that these can be approximated by the resulting changes in surface albedo, evapotranspiration and the corresponding sensitivity parameters. Here, the assumption is that, if roughness changes have any discernible large-scale effect, increases should lead to a surface cooling since they increase the turbulent exchange, hence, the amount of energy expended in the latent and sensible heat fluxes rather than in heating the surface. Thus, for the perturbed simulations, we simply double the roughness that the model simulates dynamically (\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
